.TH DESKTOP 1 "" "hibr @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
desktop \- hibr's text-mode desktop, held so it can always be detached from
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B desktop
.RB [ \-r | \-\-resume ]
.RB [ \-s | \-\-session
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-j | \-\-join ]
.RB [ \-w | \-\-standby ]
.RB [ \-n | \-\-name
.IR name ]
.br
.B desktop
.BR \-h " | " \-\-help
.br
.B desktop ctl
.RB [ \-s | \-\-session
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-S | \-\-socket
.IR path ]
.I request
.RI [ arg ...]
.br
.B desktop ctl
.RB [ \-s | \-\-session
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-S | \-\-socket
.IR path ]
.BR \-j | \-\-json
.I json
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B desktop
starts hibr's desktop in the terminal it is run from: windows, a menu bar,
desktop icons and a Control Panel, drawn in the terminal's own cells and,
where the terminal can show them, in pixels. It is a hibr script
.RI ( session.hibr
in the desktop's folder), run by
.BR hibr (1).
.PP
Every desktop is
.IR held :
it runs inside a session of hibr's
.B hold
module, so it outlives the terminal. Detach, from the hibr menu or with
ctrl-\e, gives the terminal back and leaves the desktop and everything in
its windows running.
.B desktop \-r
in any terminal comes back to it. Without
.BR \-r ,
a desktop already running under the same session name is left alone and
the command says so.
.PP
Restart Desktop, on the hibr menu, starts the desktop again on whatever
hibr is installed now and keeps the programs running in its terminal
windows. Since 0.99.166 it also upgrades the session's own server when that
is older than the hibr on disk.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-resume
Reattach to the desktop if it is already running, or start one if it is
not.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-session " \fIname\fR"
Which held session this is, for keeping more than one desktop side by side.
The default is
.BR desktop .
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-join
Attach beside an already running desktop as a further display, instead of
starting or resuming one. The desktop then spans both terminals.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-standby
Wait to be joined, now and again each time this display is let go.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " \fIname\fR"
Name this display, as the Control Panel's Displays pane and
.B desktop ctl displays
show it. Without it a display is named for where its terminal is: the
machine an ssh connection comes from, by its short name where it resolves
and its address otherwise
.RB ( SSH_CONNECTION ),
else the terminal program
.RB ( TERM_PROGRAM ),
else this machine's
.BR HOSTNAME ;
a second display with the name already attached is
.IB name #2\fR,
then
.BR #3 .
So two terminals on one machine share a name, and what it remembers (its
wallpaper among them), unless one is given
.BR \-\-name .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a summary of these options, and exit.
.SH KEYS
These are the defaults. Each one can be changed in Control Panel >
Shortcuts, which lists every action that has a key, a few more with none
by default. Every other key goes to the focused window, so a
program in a terminal window gets its own ctrl-c.
.TP
.BR F10 " or " escape
Open the menu bar. Arrows and letters then work its menus.
.TP
.BR alt-tab ", " alt-shift-tab
The window switcher: the window list with the next window highlighted,
stepped forward or back; a pause, or any other key, goes to the window it
stands on, and escape goes nowhere.
.TP
.B alt-w
Close the focused window.
.TP
.B alt-enter
Full Screen: the focused window, if it may be zoomed, takes every cell of its display, with no
bar, frame or shadow; again puts it back. It also ends when the window
loses the focus, or a menu, the screen saver or the lock comes up.
.TP
.B ctrl-f11
Leave Full Screen, which no window can take for itself.
.TP
.B ctrl-f10
Release Pointer: take the pointer back from a window holding it, which no
window can take for itself either. A window may take the pointer only within
two seconds of a key or a click, so one restored at login, a timer or a job
cannot take it by itself; a refusal is a
.B pointer lock: refused
line in the desktop's log.
.TP
.B ctrl-\e
Detach.
.TP
.BR alt-up ", " alt-down
Zoom the focused window, and minimise it.
.TP
.BR alt-left ", " alt-right ", " ctrl-alt-up ", " ctrl-alt-down
Snap the focused window to the left, right, top or bottom half.
.TP
.BR alt-1 ", " alt-2 ", " alt-3
Go to that workspace;
.BR ctrl-alt-left " and " ctrl-alt-right
go to the previous and the next.
.TP
.BR alt-c ", " alt-x ", " alt-v
Copy, Cut and Paste, through the focused window.
.TP
.BR alt-z ", " alt-y ", " ctrl-s
Undo, Redo and Save, in a window that has them.
.TP
.B alt-q
Quit the focused application: close every window it has.
.TP
.BR shift-pageup ", " shift-pagedown
Page a terminal window back through what scrolled off, and on again.
.TP
.B ctrl-a
Select every icon on the desktop.
.TP
.B alt-s
Give the keys to the Control Strip.
.TP
.B ctrl-alt-g
Screenshot.
.TP
.B ctrl-alt-r
Refresh Desktop: draw the whole screen again.
.TP
.B ctrl-alt-l
Lock the screen.
.TP
.BR alt-= ", " alt-\- ", " alt-0
Volume up, volume down, and mute.
.PP
The alt in these, except beside ctrl, is Control Panel > Shortcuts >
Shortcut Key, which can be Alt, Super or Alt, or Super; a ctrl-alt key
stays ctrl-alt whichever it is.
.PP
Quit, on the hibr menu, ends the desktop and its windows. A window is
moved by its title bar, or by alt and a drag anywhere in it.
.SH CONTROL
.B desktop ctl
asks a running desktop something through its control socket, or has it do
something, and prints the line of JSON it answers. The desktop is the
session given by
.BR \-\-session ,
else the held session the shell runs in, else
.BR desktop .
.B \-\-socket
names the socket itself, and
.B \-j
sends a request written as JSON as it is. The requests:
.TP
.B displays
The displays attached: each one's name, place and size, whether it is the
primary, what it draws, and whether the wallpaper goes to it as pixels or
cells; and the session's own answer to what the terminal draws.
.TP
.B windows
The windows: id, title, application, place, size, workspace, and whether
each is on every workspace, hidden, and has focus.
.TP
.B session
The session's name, whether it is held, the focused window, the current
workspace and how many there are, and the screen's size.
.TP
.B rescan
Read the application folders again, as the catalogue asks after it installs
or removes something.
.TP
.B state
What the desktop waits on: its process id, whether the screen saver or the
lock is up, each watcher, and the descriptors the console wakes for.
.TP
.B signedin
The person has just signed in at the login screen, which resumes this
desktop: the lock goes, the screen saver stops and the idle clock starts
again. The login sends it itself, so the password is not asked twice.
.TP
.BR "stats " [ reset | stop ]
What the desktop has done since the last reset, counted the same way on
every platform:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
its own CPU time and that of the programs it ran;
.IP \(bu 2
the wakes, frames and events since;
.IP \(bu 2
how often the sound, network and Bluetooth layers re-read their servers;
.IP \(bu 2
the bytes the console sent, and, for a held desktop, the bytes and frames
sent to each display;
.IP \(bu 2
how often the img module kept the wallpaper rather than placing it again,
and why it last could not;
.IP \(bu 2
while timing is on, from a reset until a stop: the runs, total and longest
milliseconds of each ticker, each app's windows
.RB ( win: \fIapp\fR),
each part of a frame
.RB ( part: \fIname\fR)
and the flush; the milliseconds spent drawing; what ended each wait; and why
each app's windows were drawn.
.RE
.IP
The CPU is counted from when this desktop started, a Restart Desktop
included. It does not count hold's server or the terminal, which are other
processes.
.TP
.BI "move " "id display"
Move a window to a display, named as
.B displays
names it.
.TP
.BI "resize " "id height width"
Resize a window, in cells.
.TP
.BI "focus " id
Raise a window and give it focus.
.B raise
is the same.
.TP
.BI "open " "path" \fR|\fPaddress
Open it in the desktop, as a program's
.B xdg\-open
does in a hibr session: a folder in Files, a file with its handler, an
address in the browser; a file with nothing to open it, or one that is a
program, opens its folder instead and is never run.
.B desktop open
is the same, and says nothing when it works.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B desktop
exits 1 when the session is already running and
.B \-r
was not given, or when
.B \-j
finds no desktop to join.
.PP
.B desktop ctl
exits 0 when the desktop answered
.BR {"ok":true} ,
1 for an error the desktop gave, and 2 when there is no desktop to ask, it
did not answer within five seconds, or the request was not one the client
knows how to send.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/hibr/desktop.json
The settings, written as the Control Panel changes them. It is a hibr
script of its own, read at each start.
.TP
.I ~/.config/hibr/
Also where applications, themes and the rest are found first, before the
installed, the system's and the shipped copies.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/hibr/
Where
.B catalogue install
puts applications, themes and the rest for you
.RB ( $XDG_DATA_HOME/hibr ).
.TP
.I /var/lib/hibr/
The system scope, where
.B catalogue \-\-system
installs for every user of the machine, read before your own installs;
.I \%$PREFIX/var/lib/hibr
when hibr was installed anywhere but
.IR /usr .
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/hibr/desktop.log
What went wrong, and what the desktop did that is worth knowing, one line
each with the local time. It becomes
.I desktop.log.1
when it grows too large.
.TP
.IB ${TMPDIR:-/tmp} /hibr\-ctl\- uid / session .ctl
The control socket, reachable only by its owner.
.TP
.I @DESKDIR@
The desktop as installed:
.IR session.hibr ,
the window manager, and what ships with it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HIBR_HOLD
Set inside a held session to the path of that session's socket, whose last
part is the session's name. A desktop started from a terminal window of
another desktop compares that name with its own session's, and so is held
on its own.
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_STATE_HOME ", " XDG_DATA_HOME
Where the settings, the log and your own installs are kept, as above.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Where the control socket's folder is made, as above.
.TP
.B HIBR_CONNTIMEO
Milliseconds
.B desktop ctl
waits for the desktop to accept its connection; 2000 when unset.
.TP
.B HIBR_GFX
.BR kitty ", " sixel " or " off :
the picture protocol to use, said outright rather than asked of the
terminal.
.TP
.B HIBR_COLOURS
.BR 24 ", " 256 ", " 16 " or " 8 :
how many colours to send, said outright rather than asked of the terminal.
.TP
.BR SSH_CONNECTION ", " TERM_PROGRAM
Name a display given no
.BR \-\-name ,
as above.
.PP
A session file may set any of the desktop's own
.B DT_
settings; most are declared with their defaults in the window manager's
.IR wm/settings.hibr ,
the rest beside the code that reads them, and described in
.I programs/desktop/README.md
in hibr's source.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hibr (1)
.PP
The desktop's own guide is
.I docs/desktop.md
in hibr's source;
.I docs/catalogue.md
covers
.BR catalogue ,
and
.I docs/please.md
covers
.BR please ,
which runs a command as root.
